Every clinician should assess a patient-function during an opioid-benefit-assessment.
Every clinician should assess a pain-control during an opioid-benefit-assessment.
Every clinician should assess a quality-of-life during an opioid-benefit-assessment.
Every clinician may ask a patient about a meaningful-functional-goal-progress during an opioid-benefit-assessment.
Every clinician may use a PEG-assessment-scale during an opioid-benefit-assessment.
If a patient has a pain-score and has a function-score and the pain-score improves by at least 30 percent and the function-score improves by at least 30 percent then the pain-score has a clinically-meaningful-improvement and the function-score has a clinically-meaningful-improvement.
Every PEG-assessment-scale includes exactly 3 assessment-items.
